  <h2>Vapor barrier, encapsulation and drainage</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Vapor barrier:</strong> polyethylene sheeting laid over the dirt floor, with seams overlapped and taped. It's the basic step that slows moisture rising from the soil.</li>
    <li><strong>Encapsulation:</strong> a heavier liner over the floor and up the walls, sealed at seams and piers, with vents and rim joists sealed, and a dehumidifier or a small supply of conditioned air to keep humidity in check.</li>
    <li><strong>Drainage:</strong> an interior perimeter drain that carries water to a sump pump. It's needed when liquid water collects; a liner alone just hides the water underneath.</li>
    <li><strong>Insulation:</strong> in a sealed crawl space, insulation usually moves from between the floor joists to the walls. Fiberglass batts that are wet or falling down should be removed.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Questions to ask a contractor</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Where is the water coming from, and how will you fix the source, not just cover it?</li>
    <li>What liner thickness are you using, and how are seams, piers and walls sealed?</li>
    <li>How will the space be dried afterward: dehumidifier, supply air from the HVAC system, or both? Who maintains it?</li>
    <li>Do you need a permit, and does the design meet local code for crawl space ventilation and combustion appliances?</li>
    <li>Is mold on the framing being cleaned, treated or removed, and is that in the written scope?</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Test for radon before sealing</h2>
  <p>Sealing a crawl space changes how air moves between the soil and the house. Where soil gas contains radon, the U.S. Department of Energy notes that air-sealing can raise indoor radon levels, so test first and plan for mitigation if levels are high.</p>

  <h2>Signs it's working</h2>
  <p>A crawl space that's under control stays below about 60% relative humidity, has no standing water after storms, and has no musty smell drifting into the house. A cheap hygrometer left in the crawl space and checked through a summer will tell you more than any sales pitch.</p>
